  abstract = {Breaking changes: The following changes, mostly due to new default density estimators, may cause some plots on sample data to change. Changes should usually be small, and generally should result in more accurate density estimation. Revert to the old behavior by setting {$<$}code{$>$}density = density\_unbounded(bandwidth = "nrd0"){$<$}/code{$>$}. {$<$}code{$>$}stat\_slabinterval(){$<$}/code{$>$} now uses {$<$}code{$>$}density\_bounded(){$<$}/code{$>$} as its default density estimator, which uses a bounded density estimator that also estimates the bounds of the data. The default bandwidth estimator is also now {$<$}code{$>$}bandwidth\_dpi(){$<$}/code{$>$}, which is the Sheather-Jones direct plug-in estimator (the same as {$<$}code{$>$}stats::bw.SJ(..., method = "dpi"){$<$}/code{$>$}). These changes may cause existing charts using densities to change; usually only slightly. These changes should be worth it, as they should drastically improve the accuracy of density estimates, especially on bounded data, and should have little noticeable impact on densities on unbounded data. {$<$}code{$>$}density\_bounded(){$<$}/code{$>$} now estimates bounds from the data when not provided (i.e. when one of {$<$}code{$>$}bounds{$<$}/code{$>$} is {$<$}code{$>$}NA{$<$}/code{$>$}). See the {$<$}code{$>$}bounder\_{$<$}/code{$>$} functions (e.g. {$<$}code{$>$}bounder\_cdf(){$<$}/code{$>$}, {$<$}code{$>$}bounder\_cooke(){$<$}/code{$>$}) for more on bounds estimation. Improved {$<$}code{$>$}Mode(){$<$}/code{$>$} and {$<$}code{$>$}hdi(){$<$}/code{$>$} estimators based on bounded density estimator. New features and enhancements: Improved {$<$}code{$>$}hdci(){$<$}/code{$>$} estimator using quantile estimation. Histograms are now implemented using {$<$}code{$>$}density\_histogram(){$<$}/code{$>$}, a histogram density estimator. Finer-grained control of bin positions is now possible using the {$<$}code{$>$}breaks{$<$}/code{$>$} argument (including the new {$<$}code{$>$}breaks\_fixed(){$<$}/code{$>$} for manually-specified bin widths) and the {$<$}code{$>$}align{$<$}/code{$>$} argument (including the new {$<$}code{$>$}align\_boundary(){$<$}/code{$>$} and {$<$}code{$>$}align\_center(){$<$}/code{$>$} for choosing how to align bin positions to reference points). (\#118) New {$<$}code{$>$}geom\_spike(){$<$}/code{$>$} and {$<$}code{$>$}stat\_spike(){$<$}/code{$>$} for adding spike annotations to slabs created with {$<$}code{$>$}geom\_slabinterval(){$<$}/code{$>$} or {$<$}code{$>$}stat\_slabinterval(){$<$}/code{$>$}. See example in {$<$}code{$>$}vignette("slabinterval"){$<$}/code{$>$}. (\#58, \#124) {$<$}code{$>$}parse\_dist(){$<$}/code{$>$} now outputs {$<$}em{$>$}distributional{$<$}/em{$>$} objects in a {$<$}code{$>$}.dist\_obj{$<$}/code{$>$} column in addition to the name-plus-arguments ({$<$}code{$>$}.dist{$<$}/code{$>$}+{$<$}code{$>$}.args{$<$}/code{$>$}) format, and these objects respect truncation parameters from prior specifications. This makes it easier to visualize standard deviation priors, for example, giving a better solution to \#20. {$<$}code{$>$}marginalize\_lkjcorr(){$<$}/code{$>$} adjusts the {$<$}code{$>$}.dist\_obj{$<$}/code{$>$} column output by {$<$}code{$>$}parse\_dist(){$<$}/code{$>$} in addition to the {$<$}code{$>$}.dist{$<$}/code{$>$} and {$<$}code{$>$}.args{$<$}/code{$>$} columns. {$<$}code{$>$}geom\_lineribbon(){$<$}/code{$>$} now obeys the {$<$}code{$>$}order{$<$}/code{$>$} aesthetic, allowing you to arbitrarily set the draw order of ribbons (\#171). Enabled by this change, {$<$}code{$>$}stat\_lineribbon(){$<$}/code{$>$} now sets {$<$}code{$>$}order = after\_stat(level){$<$}/code{$>$} by default, making its draw order more correct by ensuring all ribbons of the same level are drawn together. Some improved error messages using {$<$}code{$>$}cli{$<$}/code{$>$}. {$<$}em{$>$}Very{$<$}/em{$>$} experimental adaptive KDE is available through the {$<$}code{$>$}adapt{$<$}/code{$>$} parameter; note that it is unsupported and both